{
  "format": "whirligig-aircraft/1",
  "name": "Hybrid Cruiser",
  "author": "whirligig",
  "version": "1",
  "notes": "A series hybrid: a small petrol engine turns a generator, the generator feeds an electrical bus, and a brushless motor on that bus turns the propeller. The engine never touches the propeller. Nothing in the engine knows it is part of a hybrid \u2014 it is the same `engine` that drives a propeller directly in the glow trainer, bolted to a shaft that happens to have a generator on it instead. The generator is the same `motor` machine as everywhere else, simply spun faster than its own back-EMF so its current comes out negative and flows the other way. The supercapacitor is the point of the layout: the engine is sized for the CRUISE load, not the climb load, and the capacitor covers the difference for the half-minute a climb lasts, then the engine refills it. Watch the bus voltage sag on the capacitor and recover. Datum is the wing quarter chord.",
